I will trust you.

33
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:55,940
That's a big act of trust on your faithfulness, folks.

34
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:03,160
So faithfulness is kind of like God's goodness.

35
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,320
Do you ever do the thing that was really popular maybe 10 years ago, 15 years ago?

36
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:09,240
You know, God is good.

37
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:12,320
And people said, and all the time.

38
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:15,900
And you do know that God is good all the time.

39
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:18,440
But many of God's good things don't feel so good.

40
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:22,000
God is faithful.

41
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:30,420
Yeah, and all the time God is, but if you're like me, sometimes we walk through the valley of the shadow of death.

42
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:37,980
And I know that I should fear no evil, but that fear tends to creep up.

43
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:49,140
Because God's faithfulness, just like his goodness, just like his justice, is not always in line with how I think it should be worked out.

44
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:55,580
D.A. Carson one time talked about justice, that we all want justice.

45
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:56,980
We all say we want justice.

46
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:58,280
And the truth is, we don't want justice.

47
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:06,880
When we are in the midst of our foibles or our missteps or, you know, big fancy words for kin, what we want is mercy.

48
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:13,660
It's when you're doing something that violates me that I want justice.

49
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:16,520
And usually I don't really want justice.

50
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:21,840
What I want is justice, as I understand it, in this particular context, relevant to you.

51
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:25,640
So faithfulness is like that.

52
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:30,320
Faithfulness is this, God's faithfulness has this eternal perspective.

53
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:34,060
God is always faithful.

54
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:41,120
And I want to unpack that, if I can, with just three simple verses today.

55
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:47,400
So I've had the privilege of spending a lot of time overseas before coming here.

56
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:54,000
I ran a program that I used to run at the seminary I taught at for pastors across the globe.

57
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,860
And part of the time we spent in Nepal working with Nepali pastors, telling them, forget about your ministry.

58
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:01,020
This is about you and Jesus.

59
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:03,240
What your church needs is you fully alive in Jesus.

60
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,980
Nepal is a hard place.

61
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:09,860
Prior to 2008, it was illegal to preach with intent to convert.

62
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:11,900
It was a Hindu nation.

63
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:15,760
And that changed in 2008 because they wanted ISM money.

64
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,260
And to get ISM money, they had to have religious freedom.

65
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:20,720
And so they declared there were a secular state.

66
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:25,940
But the reality was, in most villages, it was still kind of illegal to preach with intent to convert.

67
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:28,000
I met a guy.

68
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,400
I don't know what his name was.

69
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:31,660
We couldn't even pronounce it.

70
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:32,380
We called him Ketchup.

71
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:35,980
This was back in the 90s, but he was from India.

72
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:37,540
He was a Sinfali.

73
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:39,740
And the Sinfalis are in India and they're in Nepal.

74
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:42,680
And he would travel across the border and preach the gospel.

75
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:50,720
And when they caught him, they took him and they handcuffed him to a chain in the police station until they could prosecute him.

76
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,520
The challenge was that he was Indian.

77
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:55,060
And Nepal didn't want to tick off.

78
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:57,000
I mean, they really don't want to tick off China or India.

79
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,460
It's this mountainous nation between the two.

80
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,180
And so they were trying to deal with him.

81
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,960
And Ketchup's testimony was, it was almost them.

82
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:07,400
For three days, I sat in the police station.

83
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:08,480
There were two chairs.

84
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:09,680
I was chained to one.

85
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:13,540
But they would bring someone else in, some criminal who would sit down and was distraught.

86
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:15,980
And I'd share him a gospel with him and he'd get saved.

87
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,840
And they'd take him away and bring me someone else.

88
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:24,040
And after three days, they finally said, okay, enough of you.

89
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:25,100
Just go back to India.

90
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:25,900
And they got rid of him.

91
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:29,400
See, that's even the faithfulness of God.

92
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:35,080
I don't know about you, but being chained to a chair doesn't sound really that glorious.

93
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:43,960
So, my good friend, James, who's planting a church in Nepal now, says our biggest challenge are what he calls miracle Christians.

94
00:05:45,020 --> 00:06:06,480
And I don't know that I met a believer who didn't in their life or their father's life or, in many cases, their grandfather's life or even their great-grandfather because it's a very collectivist society.

95
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:10,700
And so, when your grandfather becomes a Christian, everyone gravitates toward that.

96
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:15,020
One of them had a miracle or had a vision or had a dream.

97
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:20,460
And it was foundational to their accepting Jesus Christ.

98
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:26,280
But he said, our challenge today is that we have these, what we call miracle Christians.

99
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:33,940
These people who hear about the miracles and they come and they want to put in their 25-rooping coin and pull the lever and out comes their miracle.

100
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:37,080
And when their miracle doesn't come out, they're gone.

101
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:46,480
And so, we need to teach our people that God's faithfulness is not like a vending machine, but God is always faithful.

102
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:48,820
He's faithful.

103
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:52,060
So, first verse.

104
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:57,000
First Thessalonians 5, 24.

105
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:57,800
No, leave it.

106
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:58,420
No, you can.

107
00:06:58,420 --> 00:06:58,820
That's fine.

108
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:02,660
First Thessalonians 5, either one.

109
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:15,100
You know, so I am going to take those guys out for lunch today or tomorrow because the sound people and the slide people can either make you sound good or make you look foolish.

110
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:18,080
So, I'm going to love on you guys.

111
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:20,140
You're going to become my best friends.

112
