00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:17,660
For not all have faith, but the Lord is faithful, and he will strengthen and protect you.

206
00:12:17,660 --> 00:12:18,680
Okay, context.

207
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:21,940
Paul is in Turkey.

208
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:23,520
Wasn't called Turkey then.

209
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,180
And he's trying to figure out where the Lord's leading him.

210
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:30,100
And the Lord gives him a vision, and it's to go to Macedonia, which is Greece.

211
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:36,320
And so he travels across the GNC or around it, and he gets over, and he goes to Philippi,

212
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:38,320
and he preaches the gospel, and he gets stuck in jail.

213
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:42,100
And God does his miraculous things, and then he has to leave.

214
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:45,100
And people accept Christ, and they have a church.

215
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:46,560
And then he goes to the capital.

216
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:48,980
He goes to a few towns, gets to the capital, Thessalonica.

217
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:53,580
And the Scripture says he preaches for three weeks in the synagogue.

218
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:56,280
Three weeks.

219
00:12:56,280 --> 00:13:03,080
He said that in response to these three weeks of preaching, a few Jews came to faith,

220
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:12,080
a whole bunch of God-fearing Gentiles, and a significant number of prominent women.

221
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:15,180
Now, step into this context with me.

222
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:18,600
You are a leader of the Jewish synagogue.

223
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:24,520
You are in a Greek contested culture.

224
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:27,320
You are ruled by Rome.

225
00:13:27,320 --> 00:13:30,040
And you're trying to build your church.

226
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:35,680
And you gather together these Greeks who become God-fearers.

227
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:40,000
They are sick and tired of their Greek gods, which are worse than humans.

228
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:45,700
And instead they say, the one true God, who is moral and right and good and faithful.

229
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:47,860
We like that idea.

230
00:13:47,860 --> 00:13:51,720
And so they're moving over to that, and they're paying attention.

231
00:13:51,720 --> 00:13:55,360
And here comes his visiting preacher, and he preaches once.

232
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:56,680
And they go, hey, that was good.

233
00:13:56,680 --> 00:13:58,040
And let him preach a second time.

234
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:02,640
And by the third time he preaches, he steals all the potential converts at the church.

235
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:07,880
And not just all the God-fearing Greeks, but a few prominent women.

236
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:10,120
Those are the folks with the bucks in their pocket.

237
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:13,340
And the Jews are picked.

238
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:19,280
And so they stir up a mob, which is a mob of evil people.

239
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:23,500
And they go to get Paul and Silas, and they can't find them.

240
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:24,620
They go to Jason's house.

241
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:27,860
Commentators think Jason was probably a believer.

242
00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:30,820
He took on a Greek name, but probably Joshua.

243
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:40,100
And since they couldn't fault Paul and Silas, they drug Jason out of his home, to the magistrates, and accused them of sedition.

244
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:44,740
This is an accusation that comes with a death penalty.

245
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:48,420
You're teaching that there's a king other than Caesar.

246
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:51,040
And we need to deal with this.

247
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:52,940
I mean, it's a mess.

248
00:14:52,940 --> 00:15:00,640
And so the brothers go find Paul and Silas and say, hey, if those things calm down, how about you guys go on to Berea?

249
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,220
And so they go on to Berea.

250
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:04,960
They go from Berea, they get to Athens.

251
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:09,720
When they're in Athens, Paul now is a little nervous because he speaks three messages.

252
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:11,380
That's it.

253
00:15:11,380 --> 00:15:13,360
He's playing through the church.

254
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:17,560
And the mob in the area are problematic.

255
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:20,360
And so he's nervous.

256
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:21,620
And so Timothy comes.

257
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:24,640
He says, hey, Timothy, go check up on them, see how it's going.

258
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:29,100
Timothy goes back and sees that the Lord is doing great stuff.

259
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:30,460
The Lord is really faithful.

260
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:32,860
Timothy comes back with a report.

261
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:38,860
And literally within probably two or three months of Paul planting the church, only preaching three messages,

262
00:15:38,860 --> 00:15:45,580
he has to now write letters to these people to make sure that they are encouraged and that they stay the course.

263
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:51,920
By the way, does anyone know what, like, first and second testimony is one of the major themes in there?

264
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:55,880
I know everyone's afraid to say it.

265
00:15:55,880 --> 00:15:58,140
So he talks a lot about the second coming.

266
00:15:59,020 --> 00:16:01,660
And actually, he corrects a lot of stuff for the second coming.

267
00:16:01,660 --> 00:16:10,560
Because these folks who heard three sermons and accept Jesus have quit their jobs and they're going up on a hill and they're waiting for Jesus to come back.

268
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:14,040
And so Timothy gets to Athens and she says, hey, Paul, it's going great.

269
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:14,600
You did well.

270
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:17,360
But remember that sermon you did on Christ's returning?

271
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:19,940
Yeah, I think they missed a few things.

272
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:26,440
And so Paul writes this letter to them to correct that and to encourage them.

273
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:37,420
And so he says, finally, brothers and sisters, pray for us that the word of the Lord will spread rapidly and be glorified just as it was also with you.

274
00:16:37,420 --> 00:16:39,040
Remember three months ago?

275
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:41,100
I preached three times.

276
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:44,060
And God left the church.

277
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:46,400
But we're trying to do that elsewhere.

278
00:16:46,400 --> 00:16:47,400
Pray.

279
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:48,980
Pray.

280
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:55,860
And that we will be rescued from troublesome and evil people just like you helped back then.

281
00:16:57,360 --> 00:16:59,060
Just like what's going on then.

282
00:16:59,060 --> 00:17:02,740
For not all have faith.

283
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:06,240
But the Lord is faithful.

284
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:08,280
And here it comes.

285
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:11,540
It doesn't say he will solve all your problems.

286
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:15,600
It doesn't say he will take you out of the problem.

287
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:19,700
He will strengthen you.

288
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:22,800
And he will protect you.

289
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:25,680
Because God is faithful.

290
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:28,580
He's faithful.

291
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:31,440
He's faithful.

292
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:35,420
Next slide.

293
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:38,340
One more verse, and I'll go through this fairly quickly.

294
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:41,680
This comes out of 2 Timothy 2, 13.

295
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:45,240
And you can read the stuff that goes before it.

296
00:17:45,240 --> 00:17:52,700
It's probably a poem or hymn that Paul has grabbed a hold of that's become important to the Christian church.

297
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:56,260
He's writing to a pastor near the end of Paul's ministry.

298
00:17:56,380 --> 00:18:00,820
He's realizing, I'm not going to be able to keep planting churches, so I better pour into some people who can do this.

299
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:07,700
And he pulls out this hymn or this poem or this statement that had become very precious to them.

300
00:18:07,700 --> 00:18:17,060
And at the very end of it, in 2 Timothy 2, 13, if we are unfaithful, he remains faithful.

301
00:18:17,060 --> 00:18:19,940
Because he cannot deny himself.

302
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:22,600
Well, I love that.

303
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:30,480
I don't know where you're going with your message, brother, but, boy, that in and of itself makes me want to be faithful.

304
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:33,720
It's profound.

305
00:18:33,720 --> 00:18:34,440
It's powerful.

306
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:45,720
Thomas Oden, theologian, says that this whole concept of theology around faithfulness fits into the category of congruence.

307
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:48,740
That God is congruent.

308
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:55,380
That who God is and what God does matches.

309
00:18:55,380 --> 00:19:01,320
God's faithfulness is based on who he is.

310
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:06,760
Because he does based on who he is.

311
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:09,120
They match.

312
00:19:10,740 --> 00:19:17,780
Any of you remember when you were a kid and you got your first big chocolate Easter bunny?

313
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:19,280
You can flip aside.

314
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:20,800
Remember that?

315
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:29,320
I mean, the basket's here and there are jelly beans and there are those little marshmallow chickens chips from there, you know.

316
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:31,280
And I don't know what else went in your teeks.

317
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:31,800
Thank you.

318
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:38,340
But then, like, I don't know if you, but I got this thing that was so big it couldn't sit in the basket.

319
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:39,840
It had to sit behind the basket.

320
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:41,940
But here's the next question.

321
00:19:41,940 --> 00:19:46,500
Do you remember when you opened it up and you first discovered, go ahead and hit the next slide, that dumb thing was hollow.

322
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:51,320
I was so freaked and bummed out.

323
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:55,780
Folks, God is not hollow.

324
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:58,020
He's congruent.

325
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:00,080
He's congruent.

326
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:03,120
Who he is is what he does.

327
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:05,380
He's faithful.

328
00:20:05,380 --> 00:20:05,980
He's faithful.

329
00:20:05,980 --> 00:20:22,840
Oden goes on to say, God's faithfulness is God's steady, reliable application of divine truth to changing, unsettling, and developing circumstances.

330
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,620
I'm going to close my section with this illustration.

331
00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:32,020
So I had the privilege of being chaplain in the U.S. Virgin Islands to National Park.

332
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:34,460
I was chaplain in Yellowstone and Peacons, the Virgin Islands.

333
00:20:34,460 --> 00:20:39,560
And Camille Bay, where I was chaplain, at the time, was owned by Lawrence Rockefeller.

334
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:49,040
And so all these high-powered people came, and they didn't pay me to be a chaplain, so I got to work at the front desk for $5 an hour and solve problems of high-powered people.

335
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:57,980
But I got to live like I made a ton of money because, you know, all the concessionaires wanted to take me out on their yachts so I could tell the people about it, taking scuba diving, snorkeling, all that kind of stuff.

336
00:20:58,500 --> 00:21:09,480
And one of my jobs was we had to get in, that's like, get on the Camille Bay Mary 2, named after Lawrence's wife, Mary, to go pick up the people who flew into St. Thomas.

337
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:10,560
We were on St. John.

338
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:11,840
It's almost all National Park.

339
00:21:11,840 --> 00:21:12,940
There is no airport.

340
00:21:12,940 --> 00:21:14,400
Well, you can land on the water.

341
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,620
We have seaplanes, but those are a little rough.

342
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:23,840
So we take the boat all the way over, and they fly in, and then they get limousined over to the dock, and they get put on their boat.

343
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:25,980
And then we bring them back across the channel.

344
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:31,160
And usually it wasn't too bad, but on certain days, it was really rough.

345
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:36,120
And you could tell how long someone had worked on the boat by how they handled the rustiness.

346
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:44,460
You know, my first time, it was like, you know, and after a while, you get to a place where you feel comfortable because you can stand there.

347
00:21:44,460 --> 00:21:54,120
But the islanders not just stood there and not just walked across the boat, even in awe of the storm, but we had to deliver rum punch to people.

348
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:56,200
They would take that rum punch.

349
00:21:56,200 --> 00:21:59,340
I would try that rum punch, and, I mean, I'd give them a half the cup.

350
00:21:59,340 --> 00:22:00,500
The rest was all over the deck.

351
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:04,840
And these dang islanders would just walk across while it was going.

352
00:22:04,840 --> 00:22:06,160
They wouldn't drop.

353
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:09,960
Not a drop would they allow to come out of their cup.

354
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:29,080
I thought it was a great illustration because God carries his cup of truth and blessing across waves, tossed decks of your life, and he's faithful to deliver it without throwing a drop.

355
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:32,280
Our God is faithful.

356
00:22:32,280 --> 00:22:34,360
He's faithful.

357
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:37,080
He's faithful.

358
00:22:37,080 --> 00:22:41,400
Ryan, come and tell us what we need to do based on that.

359
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:41,980
Thank you.

360
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:52,000
Well, if you haven't met me yet, my name is Ryan.

361
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:53,080
Excited to be here.

362
00:22:54,320 --> 00:22:55,020
Do me a favor.

363
00:22:55,020 --> 00:22:58,980
If you've experienced God's faithfulness, would you give me a confident amen?

364
00:22:58,980 --> 00:22:59,760
Amen.

365
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:02,980
Camus, we say good word.

366
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:06,280
So, you have to get used for that a little bit.

367
00:23:06,280 --> 00:23:08,540
It just means God is true.

368
00:23:08,600 --> 00:23:15,880
Whenever I first met Matt, we were at a prayer meeting a couple weeks afterwards, and we were in prayer, and I said, good word, good word.

369
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:19,920
And afterwards, he said, is that just the trendy, new hip way of saying amen?

370
00:23:19,920 --> 00:23:21,880
I said, yes, sir.

371
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:22,720
And he said, all right.

372
00:23:22,720 --> 00:23:26,500
So, groovy, cool, amen, good word.

373
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:35,360
No, when Matt and I talked about talking about God's faithfulness, it fits in a series that we've been doing at Canvas on the fruit of the Spirit.

374
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:41,300
And we said it's extremely important that we talk about God's faithfulness before we talk about our faithfulness.

375
00:23:41,300 --> 00:23:50,100
Because that's the point of the illustration of the fruit of the Spirit, is that those characteristics don't flow from us, that they flow from God.

376
00:23:50,100 --> 00:23:55,960
And so, if we're going to be faithful to God, it has to be rooted in God's faithfulness to us.

377
00:23:55,960 --> 00:24:05,140
We can't be faithful to God without first being totally reliant and dependent on his faithfulness to us.

378
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:09,960
God has been faithful, and we respond in faithfulness.

379
00:24:09,960 --> 00:24:15,760
And throughout this series, it's kind of unique for me, because I call myself a teacher more than a preacher.

380
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:17,520
I like to go through passages of Scripture.

381
00:24:17,520 --> 00:24:20,920
And so, each week in the fruit of the Spirit, I've kind of just started with one word.

382
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:27,700
And oftentimes, I feel like I come into that study with a pretty decent understanding of what the word is.

383
00:24:27,700 --> 00:24:29,620
You know, these are well-known biblical words.

384
00:24:30,280 --> 00:24:39,380
But what I've realized throughout this study is, oftentimes, we come to these words with preconceived notions, cultural baggage, that we think is tied to a specific word.

385
00:24:39,380 --> 00:24:43,240
And then I come out of it kind of looking at it from a different angle or a different perspective.

386
00:24:43,240 --> 00:24:47,000
And the same was true this week as I studied faithfulness.

387
00:24:47,060 --> 00:24:54,140
You know, I tend to think about faithfulness as consistency, trustworthiness, kind of a solid rock.

388
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:58,400
What Eugene Peterson would call a long obedience in the same direction.

389
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:09,180
And although that is ultimately the visible fruit of faithfulness in our life, like, faithfulness in our life will manifest itself as a long obedience in the same direction.

390
00:25:09,180 --> 00:25:16,700
The actual definition of the word faithfulness, when it comes to our faithfulness to God, is all about, it's about persuasion.

391
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:19,760
It's about confidence.

392
00:25:19,760 --> 00:25:22,120
It's about being convinced of something.

393
00:25:23,860 --> 00:25:39,560
And so our faithfulness to God manifests itself in obedience, but the obedience flows out of us being persuaded, us believing, us being convinced that God is faithful, that God keeps his promises.

394
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:47,500
And so I'm really quickly, we don't have a lot of time, and Matt and I decided this week that it's harder to write a 15-minute sermon than it is a 35-minute sermon.

395
00:25:47,500 --> 00:25:48,660
So we're going to do our best here.

396
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:57,020
But my goal here, really quickly, is to define what faithfulness is, and then do some really practical application out of it.

397
00:25:57,020 --> 00:26:00,060
And so I would define Christian faithfulness this way.

398
00:26:00,060 --> 00:26:05,080
It is the visible expression that you are convinced that God keeps his promises.

399
00:26:05,080 --> 00:26:07,220
It's a fruit.

400
00:26:07,220 --> 00:26:11,220
It's a manifestation of you believing something to be true about God.

401
00:26:11,220 --> 00:26:15,580
All of the things that Matt just said about God, if you are convinced that that is true,

402
00:26:15,580 --> 00:26:19,640
your life will produce the fruit of faithfulness.

403
00:26:19,640 --> 00:26:29,160
Christian faithfulness is a pattern of living that demonstrates we have been persuaded to believe and are totally convinced that God is a God of his word,

404
00:26:29,160 --> 00:26:32,040
that he is faithful, and that he keeps his promises.

405
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:38,000
And the best way I could think to illustrate this, how many of you were strong in science in the school?

406
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:38,520
Anybody?

407
00:26:38,520 --> 00:26:39,920
We had some strong science people.

408
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:40,740
Some of you.

409
00:26:40,740 --> 00:26:43,820
Science was by far my worst subject.

410
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:45,520
I didn't get it.

411
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:46,300
I didn't track with it.

412
00:26:46,300 --> 00:26:47,640
I struggled all the way through.

413
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:53,200
But the one thing I walked away with was kind of just your general basic understanding of the scientific method.

414
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:55,140
You guys know how the scientific method works?

415
00:26:55,140 --> 00:26:58,880
Science is kind of based on experiments.

416
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:00,940
You come to an experiment with a theory.

417
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:02,260
You think you know how something works.

418
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:03,280
And then you test it.

419
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:04,520
You repeat it over and over again.

420
00:27:04,520 --> 00:27:07,380
It's about repeatable, observable experiments.

421
00:27:08,020 --> 00:27:15,820
And then ultimately you live your life, you live your life in light of what you found out to be true in the scientific process.

422
00:27:15,820 --> 00:27:21,840
And so this still affects my life every day because I am petrified of heights.

423
00:27:23,920 --> 00:27:29,540
I know you might think that that's kind of a bad fear, but actually it's based on the scientific method.

424
00:27:29,540 --> 00:27:38,080
I have observably repeated that when things fall from high heights, the thing that fell gets squished.

425
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:42,360
That's an experience that I have had on a regular basis.

426
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:47,560
I've been able to watch over and over and over again that when things fall from high places, they get squished.

427
00:27:47,740 --> 00:27:51,360
Therefore, I avoid being anywhere near a high place.

428
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:53,120
My mom is in the back of the room.

429
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:58,780
When we go to hotels on vacation, we request that you give us the lowest hotel room possible.

430
00:27:58,780 --> 00:28:02,220
Because if it's like over three stories on the balcony, I'm out.

431
00:28:02,220 --> 00:28:03,000
I'm not doing it.

432
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:04,100
Because why?

433
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:06,120
I've observed a repeatable process.

434
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:10,540
And it's impacted the way that I live my life.

435
00:28:10,540 --> 00:28:12,880
This is Christian faithfulness.

436
00:28:14,580 --> 00:28:21,460
We've observed repeatedly God being faithful over and over again.

437
00:28:21,460 --> 00:28:25,920
And because we are convinced, we are persuaded that God is a God who keeps his promises.

438
00:28:25,920 --> 00:28:30,320
It impacts the way we live our daily lives.

439
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:34,360
First, we see God's faithfulness all throughout Scripture.

440
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:41,280
The nation of Israel is on repeat, just experiencing God's faithfulness, even though they were unfaithful.

441
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:48,180
And then, for many of us, we've experienced the faithfulness of God in our lives.

442
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:52,280
Marissa and I have moved three times in our marriage over eight years.

443
00:28:52,280 --> 00:28:57,120
And almost every time, there's this situation where we're just not even sure if we're going to know where we're going to live.

444
00:28:57,120 --> 00:29:00,820
It's like we're selling a house, and we have a dog that destroys everything.

445
00:29:00,820 --> 00:29:02,800
And finding a place to live is really difficult.

446
00:29:02,920 --> 00:29:13,260
And the amount of times where we were, like, days away from having no place to live, we would remind ourselves, God's been faithful in the past.

447
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,480
He's going to be faithful in the future.

448
00:29:15,480 --> 00:29:16,500
We can trust that.

449
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:18,720
We can believe that because we've seen it on repeat.

450
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,140
We've seen God show up in big ways.

451
00:29:21,140 --> 00:29:22,560
God has been faithful to us.

452
00:29:22,560 --> 00:29:24,520
We know that he will continue to be faithful.

453
00:29:24,620 --> 00:29:28,060
We've been convinced and persuaded that God is faithful.

454
00:29:28,060 --> 00:29:30,360
And so our lives respond in that way.

455
00:29:30,360 --> 00:29:32,060
I love what Hebrews chapter 10 says.

456
00:29:32,060 --> 00:29:39,900
It says, let us hold tightly without wavering this hope that we affirm.

457
00:29:39,900 --> 00:29:41,640
We're convinced of.

458
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:46,000
For God can be trusted to keep his promises.

459
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:51,020
Therefore, it impacts the way we live.

460
00:29:51,020 --> 00:29:55,920
Let us think of ways to motivate one another to acts of love and good works.

461
00:29:55,920 --> 00:29:59,860
Let us think of ways to obey the greatest commandment of the faithful one.

462
00:29:59,860 --> 00:30:01,600
Because we know he's faithful.

463
00:30:01,600 --> 00:30:02,280
We're convinced.

464
00:30:02,280 --> 00:30:03,320
We're persuaded of that.

465
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:04,640
So we're going to live in light of that.

466
00:30:04,640 --> 00:30:07,780
Let's motivate one another to acts of love and good works.

467
00:30:08,120 --> 00:30:10,940
Let us not neglect our meeting together as some people do.

